.TH ACPID 4
.SH NAME
acpid \- the ACPI server: power, battery, AC, lid, buttons (port "acpi")
.SH SYNOPSIS
Port
.BR acpi .
x86-64 only.
.B /bin/acpid
is started at boot by
.BR init (8),
with device rights; with no ACPI tables it ends with status 0.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B acpid
reads the firmware's ACPI tables
.RB ( sys_acpi (2))
with the AML interpreter (user/lib/aml.c) and handles the machine's ACPI
events.
.TP
.B sleep types
\e_S5 and \e_S3 go to the kernel
.RB ( sys_power (2),
POWER_SET), which powers off or suspends to RAM by writing them to the PM1
control registers. Before either, the firmware is told (_PTS); after waking,
_WAK.
.BR poweroff ,
.BR reboot ,
.B suspend
and the desktop come here, through the library's
.BR power ()
(files committed first).
.TP
.B events
the SCI interrupt: the fixed power button, general-purpose events (their
_Lxx and _Exx methods), the embedded controller's queries (_Qxx). Notify()
says what changed: the battery, the charger, the lid, a button.
.TP
.B battery and AC
_STA, _BIX or _BIF, _BST; _PSR; the lid's _LID. Read at start, after each
Notify and every 30 s. On a Surface, the battery and charger come from the
Surface Aggregator over a UART.
.TP
.B the power button
powers off, as
.B poweroff
does. The lid only reports: the desktop decides (screen off, or sleep).
.TP
.B waking from S3
the power button, the GPEs of the lid, power button and controller that can
wake (their _PRW), and for
.BI suspend " N"
the CMOS clock's alarm.
.PP
Every step is logged, so a laptop's log says what was found and what
happened.
.SH MESSAGES
.TP
.B ACPI_POWER
(root) w[1] = POWER_OFF, POWER_RESTART or POWER_SLEEP (| N << 8: the clock
wakes the machine after N s too, N < 3600) \-> 0 once awake again.
.TP
.B ACPI_STATUS
rbuf <\- acpi_status_t: a change counter, flags (ACPI_AC, ACPI_BAT,
ACPI_CHARGING, ACPI_LID, ACPI_LID_CLOSED, ACPI_MWH, ACPI_S3), the charge in
%, minutes to empty or full (\-1: unknown), the rate, the capacities now,
full and design, the voltage, the times woken. \-> 0.
.TP
.B ACPI_WAIT
w[1] = the counter last seen: the reply waits until the status changes.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR battery (1),
.BR poweroff (8),
.BR sys_power (2),
.BR i2chid (4)
.SH BUGS
Machines without \e_S3 ("Modern Standby" only) would sleep in s2idle, which
SIEOS does not do yet:
.B suspend
then says so at once.
